{"id": 58, "ts": 1781729641.4675765, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the best overall fit. As the panel has highlighted, C is documented as **100\u202f% reliable**, **creates a positive crew atmosphere**, **keeps calm in a crisis**, and **makes correct decisions quickly**\u2014all core safety\u2011critical criteria (operational reliability, stress resilience, decision quality). His **understanding of complicated technology** further confirms strong technical and cognitive competence.\nThe remaining concerns\u2014difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric attitude, and limited willingness for further education\u2014are important but can be addressed through targeted cockpit\u2011communication training and a development plan. In contrast, Candidate\u202fB\u2019s **weak memory for numbers** directly threatens the numeric accuracy required for fuel and altitude calculations, and we still lack any evidence of his decision\u2011quality in safety\u2011critical situations. Candidate\u202fD, while responsible and problem\u2011solving, has no observed decision\u2011quality or numeric\u2011accuracy data, and his \u201cmoody\u201d and \u201cloner\u201d reputation raises crew\u2011cooperation risks. Candidate\u202fA lacks any concrete evidence of reliability, stress resilience, or crew cooperation.\nGiven the concrete evidence supporting C\u2019s safety\u2011critical performance and the mitigable nature of his softer\u2011skill gaps, I continue to recommend **Candidate\u202fC** for the long\u2011distance pilot position."}
